Answer: Method overloading can be achieved in three ways: Different number of parameters: Methods have different numbers of parameters. Different types of parameters: Methods have the same number of parameters but different data types. Different order of parameters: Methods have the same parameter types but in a different order. The return type alone cannot be used to overload a method. Example:

```java
class Calculator {
int add(int a, int b) {
return a + b;
}
int add(int a, int b, int c) {
return a + b + c;
}
double add(double a, double b) {
return a + b;
}
public static void main(String[] args) {
Calculator c = new Calculator();
System.out.println(c.add(10, 20));
System.out.println(c.add(10, 20, 30));
System.out.println(c.add(10.5, 20.5));
}
}
```

Output: 30 60 31.0
